# Versioning & release tags
{% if ci_shape == 'none' %}
This repo has no pipeline (CI shape `none`), so nothing tags it automatically.
Tag by hand if you want release markers, and read the rest of this page for what
a version means here.
{% else %}
Merging to `main` reads the conventional commits since the last tag, decides the
bump, and creates the tag **and** the release with generated notes in one API
call. Nothing releasable means no tag and a green pipeline, so re-running on an
already-released commit is a no-op.

| commit subject | bump |
|---|---|
| `feat:` | MINOR |
| `fix:` / `perf:` / `refactor:` | PATCH |
| any `type!:`, or a `BREAKING CHANGE:` trailer | MAJOR — MINOR while 0.x |
| `docs:` `ci:` `build:` `test:` `chore:` `style:` `revert:` | none — listed in the notes, never releases on its own |

The bump comes from the commit **subject**, so a breaking change must be written
`feat!:` (or carry a `BREAKING CHANGE:` trailer) or it ships as a patch and
nobody is warned. While the version is 0.x a break bumps MINOR rather than
cutting 1.0.0 (`major_on_zero` stays false); calling this 1.0.0 is a deliberate
act.
{% endif %}
## What a version means here

Your API is **your app**: its HTTP routes, its config keys, its database schema,
the environment variables the Deployment reads. The file layout of this repo is
not an API — it is yours to change.

**The image tag is what deploys, not the git tag.**
{%- if enable_image_build and ci_shape != 'none' %} The CI build pushes
`{{ registry_host }}/{{ git_namespace }}/{{ app_slug }}:<short-sha>`, and
{% elif enable_image_build %} With no pipeline, you build
`{{ registry_host }}/{{ git_namespace }}/{{ app_slug }}` yourself (`task build`
builds it locally) and push the tag, and
{% else %} {% endif %}`kubernetes/flux/deployment.yaml` points at a tag you
choose. A release tag is a label on the commit — a marker for the notes and for
rollback; it does not by itself change what is running. Point `image:` at the
digest or the tag the released commit produced.
{% if ci_shape == 'gitlab_selfhosted' %}
## The library pin

Every `include:` in `.gitlab-ci.yml` pins a release tag of `{{ lib_project }}`,
and moving those pins changes what gates this repo. The tag lives in **one** place —
`variables.WEISSSRV_LIB_REF`. GitLab forbids a variable in `include: ref:`, so
`scripts/check-lib-pins.py --fix` syncs those literals and the `lib-pin-check`
job fails the pipeline on any that drift or pin a branch. A bump is one edit plus
`--fix`, and re-vendoring `scripts/check-lib-pins.py`,
`scripts/check-doc-links.py` and `scripts/semantic-release.py` from the new tag
in the same {{ change_request }}.
{%- elif ci_shape == 'github' %}
## Vendored workflows

`.github/workflows/` is vendored from the shared library rather than referenced,
because GitHub has no equivalent of GitLab's `include: project:` for a private
library. Tool versions and their sha256 pins are literals there; re-vendor the
three files from the library tag you move to, in one pull request.
{%- endif %}

## Updating from the template

This repo was generated by copier and records its answers in
`.copier-answers.yml`. `copier update` replays them against a newer template
tag, so a template fix reaches this repo as a reviewable diff. Flipping an
answer (say `enable_hpa`) is the same operation — pass `--data enable_hpa=true`
or answer the prompt again.
